From 76e3ffa400e80611347e68b10d0b4b9cf8c159a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:27:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: walk a nested startup-file shell's
 input with unknown alias names (PR #2358 review 5894325109 nit 1)

A nested login/interactive shell, zsh, an ssh remote shell or a root login
shell sources its own startup files, so the session's alias names say nothing
about its aliases. Heredoc bodies fed to it (any depth), its -c payload and
its script are walked again with alias_names=None, so any NAME ( there may
open a keyword-led subshell. Quoted interpreter bodies inside it
(python3 - <<'PY') stay masked, keeping the ssh 'bash -ls' repro allowed.

The detector lives in cron/lifecycle_guard_nested_shells.py (FILE_LINES cap).
After #2494 (915aebe6ac, clustered -c flags) `_shell_options_end` returns the
payload operand's index for a -c, so `_shell_inputs` reads arguments[index];
clustered shapes (bash -lc, -ilc, -euo pipefail -lc, -c -l, zsh -lc/-ec) are
covered by the nested-shell tests.

RED->GREEN: on the #2358 source 18 new session-alias tests fail (including
the exact nit shape `bash -l <<'EOF'\nt ( bash ~/.hermes/kanban.db )\nEOF`
with session aliases {"zz"}); with this change all pass. Guard/heredoc test
files (29 files referencing lifecycle_guard/shell_heredoc/terminal_tool_guards):
1953 passed.

Replay over 27728 unique real terminal commands from state.db x 5 alias sets
(none/ll/stock/zz/unknown), old (main 1cd1ecc706) vs new: 0 verdict changes;
refused 28/28/29/28/55 both sides (identical JSON output).
